From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Faust <willtechnoduck@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 02:35:21 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] FEX: wake the dormant Mono backpatcher optimisation on iOS

HandleRWXAccessViolation already recognises Mono's XCHG patcher and recompiles
that block so the store becomes a direct MonoBackpatcherWrite call, with no
fault at all. On iOS it had never once fired: [mono-cfg] reported HOOKS ARMED
while "Detected mono backpatcher" appeared zero times in every run, because our
Mach handler emulates the SWPAL before FEX is ever offered the fault.

This adds the FEX half of the bridge.

IosMonoBridge.h is a byte-for-byte copy of the unix-side contract. The two live
in different build systems with no shared include path, so abi_version is the
runtime guard - FEX refuses to arm rather than read a drifted layout, which
matters because the native side reads that struct inside a fault handler.

IosJitAlias.cpp owns the table, for the same reason it owns IosAliasEntries:
reaching global storage from ARM64EC class methods in Module.cpp produces
misaligned ldr/str link errors. IosMonoResolveRW is a sequence-lock read that
mirrors the writer exactly - sample generation, read, sample again, accept only
if equal and odd - so a retired-and-reused slot misses instead of returning a
mapping that no longer backs the address.

Core.cpp publishes the CpuStateFrame and JIT block header/tail offsets so the
native capture is self-calibrating; nothing is hardcoded across the boundary.
IosMonoTryActivate consumes a pending event at CompileBlock. ios_fex_rip_from_hostpc
is used ONLY to prove the faulting instruction is inside Mono and really is an
XCHG; MarkMonoBackpatcherBlock receives JITCodeTail.RIP, the block entry, because
FEX compares it against the compilation's starting GuestRIP. In the field those
two differed by 0xC0 - passing the instruction RIP would have compiled, shipped,
and silently done nothing.

MonoBackpatcherWrite now resolves the destination to the writable alias. Its
plain store to the R+X guest VA would Mach-fault straight back on iOS, trading
one fault for another. A resolve miss is counted and falls through to the direct
store: degraded, never wrong.

DisableSMCDetection is skipped on iOS. It reprotects every RWX interval as
writable, and iOS will never grant write on the guest VA - that is the entire
reason the RW alias exists.

